cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\sin 40^\\circ \\cdot \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"8bac3748299c24552d468c80fa76cec350740a03875628e4c7881944940d521d"},"seconds":68.97658777236938,"seed":186021987540501677,"stop":"eos","text":"To simplify the expression \\(\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ\\), we can use a series of trigonometric identities and properties. Let's proceed step by step.\n\n1. **Express \\(\\cos 30^\\circ\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Use the identity \\(\\cos x = \\sin (90^\\circ - x)\\) for \\(\\cos 70^\\circ\\) and \\(\\cos 50^\\circ\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\cos 70^\\circ = \\sin 20^\\circ, \\quad \\cos 50^\\circ = \\sin 40^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n3. **Rewrite the expression using these identities:**\n   \\[\n   \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ = \\cos 10^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\sin 40^\\circ \\cdot \\sin 20^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n4. **Use the product-to-sum identities for \\(\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (40^\\circ - 20^\\circ) - \\cos (40^\\circ + 20^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 20^\\circ - \\cos 60^\\circ]\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\cos 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Substitute this back into the expression:**\n   \\[\n   \\cos 10^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ\\right)\n   \\]\n\n6. **Simplify further:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n7. **Use the product-to-sum identity for \\(\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (10^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos (10^\\circ - 20^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 30^\\circ + \\cos (-10^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\cos 10^\\circ\\right)\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\cos 10^\\circ\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\cos 10^\\circ\\right)\n   \\]\n\n8. **Combine all terms:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\cos 10^\\circ\\right) - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{3}{16}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the simplified value is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{16}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{3}{16}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To simplify the expression \\(\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ\\), we can use a series of trigonometric identities and properties. Let's proceed step by step.\n\n1. **Express \\(\\cos 30^\\circ\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Use the identity \\(\\cos x = \\sin (90^\\circ - x)\\) for \\(\\cos 70^\\circ\\) and \\(\\cos 50^\\circ\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\cos 70^\\circ = \\sin 20^\\circ, \\quad \\cos 50^\\circ = \\sin 40^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n3. **Rewrite the expression using these identities:**\n   \\[\n   \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ = \\cos 10^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\sin 40^\\circ \\cdot \\sin 20^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n4. **Use the product-to-sum identities for \\(\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (40^\\circ - 20^\\circ) - \\cos (40^\\circ + 20^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 20^\\circ - \\cos 60^\\circ]\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\cos 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Substitute this back into the expression:**\n   \\[\n   \\cos 10^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ\\right)\n   \\]\n\n6. **Simplify further:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n7. **Use the product-to-sum identity for \\(\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (10^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos (10^\\circ - 20^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 30^\\circ + \\cos (-10^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\cos 10^\\circ\\right)\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\cos 10^\\circ\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\cos 10^\\circ\\right)\n   \\]\n\n8. **Combine all terms:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\cos 10^\\circ\\right) - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{3}{16}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the simplified value is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{16}}\n\\]","id":"precalculus:405"}
